Checks a document or a directory containing documents

Usage: sprocket check [OPTIONS] [SOURCE]...

Arguments:
  [SOURCE]...
          A set of source documents as files, directories, or URLs

Options:
  -o, --off <RULE>
          Disables an analysis or lint rule if it would have been included otherwise.

          Repeat the flag multiple times to except multiple rules or tags. This is additive with rules turned off in config files.

      --warn <RULE>
          Sets a rule's severity to `warning`.

          Repeat the flag to set multiple rules. This takes precedence over the severity in config files and over `--note`. It does not enable a rule that is disabled via `--off`.

      --note <RULE>
          Sets a rule's severity to `note`.

          Repeat the flag to set multiple rules. This takes precedence over the severity in config files. It does not enable a rule that is disabled via `--off`.

      --deny-warnings
          Causes the command to fail if warnings were reported

      --deny-notes
          Causes the command to fail if notes were reported.

          Implies `--deny-warnings`.

      --suppress-imports
          Suppress diagnostics from documents that were not explicitly provided in the sources list (i.e., were imported from a provided source).

          If the sources list contains a directory, an error will be raised.

      --show-remote-diagnostics
          Show diagnostics for remote documents.

          By default, when checking a local document remote diagnostics are suppressed. This flag will show diagnostics for remote documents. This flag has no effect when checking a remote document.

      --hide-notes
          Hide diagnostics with `note` severity

      --hide-warnings
          Hide diagnostics with `warning` or `note` severity

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase logging verbosity

  -q, --quiet...
          Decrease logging verbosity

      --no-ignore
          Ignore `.sprocketignore` files while discovering WDL documents

      --color <COLOR>
          Controls output colorization

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Automatically colorize output depending on output device
          - always: Always colorize output
          - never:  Never colorize output

          [default: auto]

  -m, --report-mode <MODE>
          The report mode for any emitted diagnostics

          Possible values:
          - full:     Prints diagnostics as multiple lines
          - one-line: Prints diagnostics as one line

  -c, --config <CONFIG>
          Path to the configuration file

  -s, --skip-config-search
          Skip searching for and loading configuration files.

          Only a configuration file specified as a command line argument will be used.

      --generate-baseline
          Generate a baseline file from current diagnostics and exit

      --no-baseline
          Ignore the baseline file for this run

  -l, --lint
          Enable lint checks in addition to validation errors

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
